{0 Handlers}

Register the event handlers that your Worker needs at the top level of its
OCaml program. {!Workero.serve} handles HTTP requests,
{!Workero.scheduled} handles cron triggers, {!Workero.queue} consumes Queue
messages, {!Workero.email} receives email and {!Workero.tail} receives the trace
items of other Workers. Each registration is independent:
a cron-only or queue-only Worker does not need an HTTP handler. Classes for
Durable Objects, entrypoints and Workflows are not event handlers. Register
them with {!Workero.Durable_object.register}, {!Workero.Entrypoint.register}
and {!Workero.Workflow.register}.

{1 Handle HTTP requests}

{!Workero.serve} takes a function with the same three arguments as a
JavaScript [fetch(request, env, ctx)] handler. It returns a
{!Workero.Response.t}. This [src/main.ml] responds to a request and reads a
variable from [env]:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let handler req env ctx =
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path (Request.url req)) with
  | GET, "/" ->
      Context.wait_until ctx (fun () ->
          Promise.await (Timer.sleep 100);
          print_endline "request answered");
      Response.text
        (Option.value (Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"hello" ^ "\n")
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.text req)
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Not_Found "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

With [GREETING = "hi"] in the [[vars]] section of [wrangler.toml],
[curl http://localhost:8787/] returns [hi]. The [env] argument also contains
resource bindings. Use functions such as {!Workero.KV.of_env} and
{!Workero.Queue.of_env} to look them up.
See {{!page-env}Variables and secrets} and {{!page-bindings}Bindings}.

{!Workero.Context.wait_until} runs work in another fiber that can continue
after the response. Its function can wait for JavaScript Promises; failures
there are logged and do not change the response. If your Worker runs on a route
with an origin,
{!Workero.Context.pass_through_on_exception} lets the origin answer when the
request handler raises. It has no effect on [workers.dev] or without an origin.
See {{!page-async}Async and background work} for waits and background work.

{1 Handle cron triggers}

Add this [src/main.ml] for a Worker that only handles cron events:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.scheduled (fun controller _env _ctx ->
      Printf.printf "cron %s scheduled for %.0f\n%!"
        (Workero.Scheduled.cron controller)
        (Workero.Scheduled.scheduled_time controller))
]}

Configure [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[triggers]
crons = ["*/5 * * * *"]
]}

{!Workero.Scheduled.scheduled_time} gives milliseconds since the Unix epoch.
With [npx wrangler dev --test-scheduled] running, use this command to fire the
trigger locally:

{@sh[
curl "http://localhost:8787/__scheduled?cron=*/5+*+*+*+*"
]}

The Worker logs a line beginning [cron */5 * * * * scheduled for ]. The
timestamp varies with the event.

{1 Consume Queue messages}

{!Workero.queue} takes a {!Workero.Queue.Batch.t}, [env] and [ctx]. It returns
[unit]. Register it at the top level, as with the other handlers. The
{{!page-queue}Queue guide} shows a producer binding, consumer configuration,
body decoding and acknowledgement in a complete Worker.

{1 Receive email}

{!Workero.email} takes a {!Workero.Email.t}, [env] and [ctx]. It returns [unit].
The handler forwards, replies to or rejects the message. The
{{!page-email}Email guide} covers each action and its limits.

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.email (fun message _env _ctx ->
      Workero.Email.forward message "me@example.org")
]}

{1 Receive trace items}

{!Workero.tail} takes a list of {!Workero.Trace.t}, [env] and [ctx]. It returns
[unit]. Register it in a Worker of its own, which a producer lists in
[tail_consumers]. The {{!page-tail}Tail Workers guide} shows the configuration
and a complete handler.

{1 Runtime behavior}

Each event handler runs in a fiber. You can call {!Workero.fetch}, bindings
and {!Workero.Promise.await} inside it while other events run. The build's
[index.js] exports only the handlers registered at startup: [fetch],
[scheduled], [queue], [email] and/or [tail]. A program with no event handlers and no
{{!page-durable_objects}Durable Object classes},
{{!page-entrypoints}entrypoint classes} or
{{!page-workflows}Workflow classes} fails Worker initialization
with [workero: the OCaml program registered no handler].

An uncaught HTTP handler exception rejects the response Promise, and the
runtime returns [500]. An uncaught cron handler exception fails that run. An
uncaught Queue handler exception fails that delivery; unacknowledged messages
can be retried. An uncaught email handler exception rejects the event, and
Cloudflare treats the delivery as failed. An uncaught tail handler exception
rejects the event; the producer is not affected. Other events continue. See
{!Workero.serve}, {!Workero.scheduled}, {!Workero.queue}, {!Workero.email} and
{!Workero.tail}
for their signatures.
